{
  "requirement": "For the records displayed by {cite}`requirements.test-coverage.index.summary`,\n`habeas mdbook` MUST generate a detail subchapter under the index chapter for\neach recorded nextest test process, identifying its binary, test name, attempt,\ntest-attempt outcome, and originating run. Each report MUST show its instrumented\nfirst-party Rust source lines and execution counts, distinguishing executed\nfrom unexecuted lines, using the source captured for that run, and link back to\nthe index.\nCoverage from associated child processes MUST remain distinguishable from\ncoverage recorded by the nextest test process itself.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed tests adequately check each obligation in `requirements.test-coverage.index.details`. I read `detail` and `populate_in` in `src/execution_coverage/book.rs` and the viewer asset `assets/coverage-viewer.js`. I used them only to see what the assertions exercise, not to judge correctness.\n\n**Obligations and the tests that cover them**\n- **One detail subchapter per recorded test process under the index.**\n  - `reports_identify_attempts_and_union_child_counts_without_discarding_profiles` asserts the index chapter has 4 `sub_items`: two attempt detail chapters plus two captured-source chapters.\n  - It checks that each \"# Test process coverage\" chapter is linked from the index by its generated href.\n- **Identification of binary, test name, attempt, outcome and originating run.**\n  - The same test asserts each detail chapter contains `Binary: <code>binary</code>`, `Attempt: <code>1</code>`, `Outcome: <code>success</code>` and `Captured at Unix ns`.\n  - It also asserts `Test: <code>one</code>` and `Run: <code>{first_run}</code>` with the actual run id.\n- **Instrumented lines and counts, executed versus unexecuted, from the source captured for that run.**\n  - The JS test `process reports preserve counters, source text, child identity and lazy file rendering` renders a report. It asserts `18446744073709551615 executed` (a full u64 counter) and `0 unexecuted`.\n  - It also asserts that the captured source `<script>captured text</script>` is shown literally.\n  - The same test rejects source messages from an unrelated window and from a different run id, so only the captured source for this run is accepted.\n  - The Rust test overwrites `code.rs` (\"rebuilt source\") and then asserts that the saved `fn one() {}` and the saved counters are still rendered, so the report uses the captured source rather than the working tree.\n  - `rendered_detail_scripts_remain_literal_across_blank_lines` renders the chapter through pulldown-cmark. It asserts the viewer script and the JSON counters survive intact.\n- **Link back to the index.** The Rust test asserts every detail chapter contains `[Back to coverage index](../coverage.html)`.\n- **Child-process coverage distinguishable from the test process's own coverage.**\n  - The Rust test asserts the embedded JSON carries `\"kind\":\"Test\"` and `\"kind\":\"Child\"`, and that the child's counts `\"1\":\"0\",\"2\":\"7\"` stay separate.\n  - The JS test asserts a separate `Child process 2` section. The child's source is not rendered until its `details` element is opened, which is also asserted.\n\n**Residual observations (not gaps)**\n- The tests never include a source line that has no instrumented location, so they would not catch a viewer that also lists uninstrumented lines.\n- The requirement does not forbid listing such lines.\n- The \"first-party\" filtering is applied when profiles are collected, before the detail stage. It is covered by tests for other requirements.\n- Detail pages for failed, interrupted or stale attempts are mostly exercised through the index assertions. This adds no obligation beyond the listed ones.",
  "references": "3cd44befda18e0f7e1c42649e29fe1481fc645ecff5dd04b3163907611e76c99",
